# Rigor core overlay — supplemental core signatures.
#
# --- Process.last_status ---
#
# CRuby's `process.c` defines `Process.last_status` as a singleton method
# answering the calling thread's last child status — the object `$?` reads:
#
#   rb_define_singleton_method(rb_mProcess, "last_status", proc_s_last_status, 0);
#
# It is `nil` until the thread has waited on a child process. Upstream
# `ruby/rbs` names `::last_status` in `Process`'s doc comment but has never
# declared it (no commit to `core/process.rbs` through 4.2 adds a `def`), so
#
#   system("true")
#   status = Process.last_status
#
# reported `call.undefined-method` on correct code. The return mirrors rbs
# core's own `$?: Process::Status?`.
#
# Unconditional: `Process` and `Process::Status` are rbs core on every
# supported release. Should an upstream release declare the method, this file
# MUST be gated off that release through `RbsLoader::RBS_LINE_CORE_OVERLAYS` —
# a second `def` is a `DuplicatedMethodDefinitionError` that takes `Process`'s
# whole singleton surface down to `Dynamic[top]`.
module Process
  def self.last_status: () -> Process::Status?
end
